## 8) Optional: make blocking conditional on the toggle

Right now, the rules are always active. If you want the toggle to actually enable/disable blocking, you should switch between enabled and disabled rules dynamically using `declarativeNetRequest.updateEnabledRulesets()`.

Here’s an improved `background.js`:

```js
const RULESET_ID = "tracker_rules";

async function applyProtectionState(enabled) {
  if (enabled) {
    await chrome.declarativeNetRequest.updateEnabledRulesets({
      enableRulesetIds: [RULESET_ID],
      disableRulesetIds: []
    });
  } else {
    await chrome.declarativeNetRequest.updateEnabledRulesets({
      enableRulesetIds: [],
      disableRulesetIds: [RULESET_ID]
    });
  }
}

chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(async () => {
  const defaults = { enabled: true };
  const { enabled } = await chrome.storage.sync.get(defaults);
  await applyProtectionState(enabled);
});

chrome.storage.onChanged.addListener(async (changes, areaName) => {
  if (areaName === "sync" && changes.enabled) {
    await applyProtectionState(changes.enabled.newValue);
  }
});
```

